  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1127 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nA cold front has cleared the region this evening, bringing heavy \nrain showers to much of the region, producing between one quarter \nand one half of an inch of rainfall. A surface high will continue \nto push its way south behind the front, leading to gusty winds in \nexcess of 20 mph. These strong winds will then advect cooler air \ninto the region with highs dropping into the 40s and 50s to start \nthe week alongside lows in the 30s through the end of the week.\n\nAloft, the pattern has started to evolve into a more typical\nearly-winter look with a more progressive and amplified\nappearance. Behind Sunday's weak upper trough and frontal\npassage, two troughs will eject out of the Rockies; one in the\nsouthern Plains and another diving south out of Canada into the\nnorthern Plains. These two features will then begin a delicate\ndance over the central CONUS through the middle of the week,\nbecoming the main focus of the forecast this week. While we await\nthese troughs through Wednesday, surface high pressure will\ncontinue to keep the cooler temperatures throughout the region\nwith dry weather.\n\nBy Wednesday, the southern trough will have made its way down to\nthe Gulf coastline while the northern one continues to trek\nsouthward through the Plains. The two troughs will then begin to\nphase/merge throughout the day. An area of enhanced, meridional\nupper flow will develop in advance of the two features,\npositioned over the Middle Mississippi River Valley. In response,\na surface trough will begin to develop across the Mid-South and\nsouth into the Gulf. Simultaneously, the confidence has continued\nto rise that the southern trough will then swing eastward,\nproducing a deep layer of DPVA-induced lift with a wide swatch of\nprecipitation developing over the surface trough. Forecast\nsoundings indicate high stability with high saturation. So,\nwidespread and stratiform rainfall will be the primary impact\nthrough Wednesday.\n\nForecast confidence drops drastically moving through Wednesday\nevening and into Thursday morning. The northern trough will drop\nsouth, close enough to attempt the aforementioned merger with the\nsouthern trough. However, models are still not yet in agreement\non how this will occur, impacting the amplitude of the broader\nupper system and the surface response. This is important since\nthe surface high over the Midwest will be capable of supporting\nlow-level temperatures around 0 C, making a case for frozen\nhydrometeors. But any winter precipitation types are heavily\ndependent on the evolution of the upper system allowing for\nproper mid-level ascent and a strong enough surface response to\nadvect sub-freezing low-level temperatures. Fortunately, the\nmajority of ensemble members and deterministic models alike do\nnot favorably evolve the upper system to support frozen\nhydrometeors Thursday morning. Regardless, any potential for snow\nis not expected to lead to any meaningful impacts and would only\nmix into ongoing rainfall, which will exit the region by Friday.\n\nWhile the chaos of the upper lows takes place Wednesday and\nThursday, a stronger surface low will develop along the\nsoutheastern Atlantic coastline from the coastal baroclinic zone.\nThe vast majority of guidance supports this development, which\nwill pull away to the northeast, bringing drier air into the\nregion by the end of the week. The length of this respite,\nhowever, is less certain due to the increasing forecast variance\nin the wake of the trough merger earlier in the week. Therefore,\nit is difficult to pin down next weekend's forecast quite yet, at\nleast in terms of potential low temperatures and precipitation.",
